$CATEGORY: $course$/Quiz 3 · Marketing mix modelling II\: many countries, one budget

// question 1
::S03 Q01::Alpenglow estimates a separate MMM for each of its six countries. Which statement about the small markets (Austria, Poland) is most accurate? {
	~Their coefficients are biased towards zero because small budgets cannot move sales.
	=Their coefficients are unbiased but have wide confidence intervals because media spend varies little relative to sales noise.
	~Their coefficients are more precise because small markets have fewer channels.
	~Their coefficients cannot be estimated without at least five years of data.
	####separate OLS is unbiased under its assumptions; the problem in small markets is low signal-to-noise, hence wide intervals, not bias.
}

// question 2
::S03 Q02::A pooled regression with country fixed effects (`C(country)`) assumes that\: {
	~each country has its own media coefficients and its own intercept.
	~all countries share the same intercept but have different media coefficients.
	=all countries share the same media coefficients; only the intercepts differ.
	~countries with more data receive a higher weight on their media coefficients.
	####fixed effects shift the level per country; slopes (media effects) are common unless interactions are added.
}

// question 3
::S03 Q03::In partial pooling (shrinkage), a country's estimate is pulled towards the cross-country mean. The pull is strongest when\: {
	~the country's own estimate is very precise and countries are very different.
	=the country's own estimate is noisy and countries appear similar.
	~the country has the largest budget.
	~the pooled estimate has a large standard error.
	####the weight on own data is tau^2 / (tau^2 + se^2); a large se and a small between-country variance tau^2 give a small weight.
}

// question 4
::S03 Q04::In a Bayesian MMM, the prior on a channel's adstock decay is best described as\: {
	~the value the data would give with an infinite sample.
	=a distribution expressing what is believed about the parameter before seeing this dataset.
	~the estimate from the pooled model.
	~a constraint that the optimiser must satisfy.
	####the prior encodes business knowledge; it is updated by the likelihood to give the posterior.
}

// question 5
::S03 Q05::The Austrian model reports P(ROAS of OOH > 1) \= 0.35. The most appropriate reading is\: {
	~OOH loses money in 65 % of weeks.
	=Given the model and data, there is a 35 % probability that OOH pays back its cost on average.
	~OOH returns EUR 0.35 per euro spent.
	~The estimate is not statistically significant, so OOH has no effect.
	####the posterior probability is a statement about the parameter, not about individual weeks or significance.
}

// question 6
::S03 Q06::For a fixed total budget and smooth, concave response curves with no binding constraints, the optimal allocation has\: {
	~equal spend in every cell.
	~equal average ROAS in every cell.
	=equal marginal ROAS in every cell.
	~all spend in the cell with the highest average ROAS.
	####if marginal returns differed, moving a euro from the lower to the higher cell would raise total revenue.
}

// question 7
::S03 Q07::An unbounded budget optimiser puts the entire Austrian budget into paid social. The most likely reason is\: {
	~paid social has the largest adstock.
	=the estimated paid social curve is nearly linear over the observed range, so the model sees no diminishing returns.
	~the optimiser has a bug.
	~paid social is the cheapest channel per impression.
	####corner solutions arise when a curve does not saturate within the data; the optimiser extrapolates the straight line.
}

// question 8
::S03 Q08::Management imposes "no country loses more than 30 % of its budget". In the optimisation this is\: {
	~a bound on each cell.
	~an equality constraint on the total.
	=an inequality constraint on the sum of a country's cells.
	~a change to the response curves.
	####country floors restrict sums over cells, implemented as inequality constraints in SLSQP.
}

// question 9
::S03 Q09::The recommended plan is evaluated under 200 coefficient draws and gains revenue in 95 % of them. Which statement is correct? {
	~The plan will gain revenue in 95 % of future weeks.
	=The plan's expected gain is robust to estimation uncertainty in the coefficients, given the assumed curve shapes.
	~The model has 95 % accuracy.
	~The gain is guaranteed as long as the budget stays fixed.
	####the draws vary the coefficients, not the functional form or future conditions; the statement is conditional on the model.
}

// question 10
::S03 Q10::Poland entered the market four years ago and the MMM says Polish TV has a low marginal ROAS. The best managerial response is\: {
	~Cut Polish TV to zero immediately.
	~Ignore the model because Poland is culturally different.
	=Treat the result as a short-term estimate, cut gradually within bounds and test, because three years of data cannot capture brand building in a young market.
	~Increase Polish TV because awareness always pays off.
	####market maturity moderates what an MMM can see; young markets need caution and experiments, not corner solutions.
}
